{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's mechanism asserts the reliability gain comes from 'Wisdom of the Crowd' statistical cancellation of individual errors facilitated by the checklist standardizing data input. But this does not answer the problem's required distinguishing evidence: it never identifies what within a single evaluation would separate a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. The claim that the checklist is merely a shared coordinate system and the real gain is crowd error-cancellation makes the checklist epiphenomenal with respect to reliability, leaving no intra-evaluation evidence proposed to distinguish substantive gain from coincidental compliance.","counterexample":["A panel of non-experts independently applies a checklist that encodes a common, objectively wrong heuristic (e.g., 'if symptom A present, conclude X'). Every member ticks the box and the panel agrees on X. Inter-rater reliability is perfect and individual idiosyncratic errors cancel, yet the judgment is wrong and reliability gain is illusory compliance—exactly the case SRC_001 fails to distinguish."],"target":"2cebb9efb2a0e170b0bcb63c73cda257bdba2fcfe073b32e52f01dde05a44a09"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 shifts the argument to peak vs average performance and black swan events, but never addresses the problem's core demand: identifying evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Its mechanism about checklist incompleteness at the limit is orthogonal; it provides no diagnostic criterion within one evaluation for separating real reliability from coincidental compliance.","counterexample":["In a single evaluation of verifiable-outcome tasks, the panel's checklist answers coincide with the right answers purely because the test set sampled only routine cases the checklist covers. SRC_002's average-vs-peak framing cannot distinguish this coincidental compliance from a genuine reliability gain, since both yield high mean accuracy on that evaluation."],"target":"4a8bdefc2bae4275d342a3028288bbd0e9cc4e9b942dab01282b0206658a7e1a"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 argues for the expert by claiming checklists cannot capture dynamic, non-linear dependencies, but it invokes a specific case (Boeing 737 MAX in SCR_006) not contained in this target's own content; treating that external reference as substantive support is unjustified. More importantly, it never identifies evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer, which the problem explicitly requires.","counterexample":["A verifiable task set involves only routine items with an exhaustive, finite failure-mode list perfectly mapped by the checklist (SRC_003's own countercondition). The panel's checklist compliance tracks the right answers, but SRC_003's dynamic-dependency argument offers no intra-evaluation evidence to show this is mere compliance rather than a genuine reliability gain."],"target":"d55dd8768a2c98bedfef1aff7072e80b2d3882dfe4fcda6b5c42c7d967792928"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004's mechanism explains why the checklist reduces expert premature closure but does not identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. Its counterconditions (black swan not captured; fatigue inducing rote ticking) identify failure modes, not the diagnostic evidence the problem asks for. The claim that the checklist eliminates premature closure does not, by itself, show the panel's correct answers are due to genuine reliability rather than coincidental compliance.","counterexample":["A panel using a checklist that happens to list the correct answer's antecedents rote-ticks every box and reaches the right answer, but not via the 'cognitive forcing' mechanism (members never actually search the problem space; they just tick). SRC_004 provides no intra-evaluation evidence to distinguish this coincidental compliance from a genuine reliability gain."],"target":"e8976ade9a349875d8bede63ec6c07ce193ed4ed667a85a4fbda2604200f30e3"}]}